    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "I collaborated with the Continuous Improvement and Rapid Cycle Learning and Evaluation (CIRCLE) team at the Stanford Center on Early Childhood in order to gain experience using a Theory of Impact (TOI) to guide a large-scale program evaluation. My project had two components. First, I contributed to the CIRCLE team’s evaluation of the Thriving Providers Project (TPP) — a first-of-its-kind program that supports home-based childcare providers with direct cash transfers in various pilot locations throughout the country. In my project, I created a codebook based on TPP’s Theory of Impact, analyzed qualitative data from providers in the Colorado pilot, and distilled my findings into narrative form. Ultimately, my work was published in August 2024 as part of CIRCLE’s 18-month impact report which can be found here: https://thrivingproviders.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/Home-Grown-TPP-18-MONTH-REPORT.pdf. Additionally, I applied what I learned about program evaluation to K-12 educational initiatives. Throughout the winter, I took part in weekly CIRCLE team meetings to discuss how technical assistance providers can build capacity in partner organizations. Using what I learned and drawing on my experience as a school leader, I created 1) a report detailing how CIRCLE’s tools could be leveraged in a local literacy initiative and 2) a manual for K-12 school leaders on how to use a TOI to guide continuous improvement of a school-site program.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
